DIRECTING THE SECRETARY OF AGRICULTURE & FORESTRY TO ESTABLISH 
THE VIRGINIA WOOD COUNCIL 
 
 
Virginia’s forests are major economic drivers for the Commonwealth. Forestry currently 
generates more than 108,000 related jobs and contributes $21 billion annually to Virginia’s 
economy. As much as 25% of revenue received from forest product sales is returned to the 
localities where the forests are located. 
 
 Federal tariffs and related market challenges are creating uncertainty for this vital 
Virginia industry. It is critical that Virginia proactively address these economic risks and ensure 
that all relevant stakeholders understand the challenges and have the opportunity to propose 
solutions.  
 
Directive 
 
Accordingly, I hereby direct the Secretary of Agriculture & Forestry, in consultation with 
the Secretary of Commerce & Trade and the Secretary of Natural & Historic Resources, to form 
the Virginia Wood Council. The Council will serve as a strategic advisory body to align key 
stakeholders in Virginia’s wood and forest products industry and address current and future 
market challenges. The Secretary of Agriculture & Forestry, or her designee, shall be the chair of 
the Virginia Wood Council. 
 
The Virginia Wood Council membership shall include representatives from across the 
forest economy, including:  
 
• Department of Forestry; 
• Department of Agriculture and Consumer Services; 
• Virginia Economic Development Partnership; 
• Virginia Department of Conservation and Recreation; and 
• Other relevant government agencies, and key industry associations.

The Secretary of Agriculture & Forestry shall encourage the following groups to 
participate as members of the Council: 
 
• Primary and secondary wood manufacturers; 
• Forest landowners; 
• Virginia-based academics;  
• Virginia Farm Bureau; 
• Virginia Forestry Association; 
• Virginia Forest Products Association; and 
• Virginia Loggers Association. 
 
The Virginia Wood Council shall: 
 
1. Support the retention, expansion, and recruitment of wood-based businesses, 
including sawmills, manufacturers, and secondary processors, within the 
Commonwealth; 
 
2. Provide technical expertise and strategic guidance on domestic and international 
market development for Virginia-made wood products; 
 
3. Identify and promote opportunities for investment, innovation, and competitive 
enhancement in the sector; 
 
4. Collaborate with public and private partners to ensure that Virginia’s forests 
remain both a sustainable resource and a driver of economic opportunity; 
 
5. Support workforce development and training programs to address labor and skill 
needs in wood manufacturing and forest operations; 
 
6. Recommend policies to strengthen supply chain infrastructure and logistics that 
benefit forest landowners, wood manufacturers and timber producers alike; and 
 
7. Assess the success and further expansion of the Virginia Grown: Forest Products 
program. 
 
The Council shall meet no fewer than three times annually and may advise on the use of 
public and private funds to support initiatives that modernize operations, build new markets, and 
align the forestry sector with Virginia’s broader economic development and branding priorities. 
 
On or before November 1, 2026, and each subsequent year, the Council shall submit an 
annual report to the Governor’s Office detailing the progress made toward the goals and 
objectives outlined in this directive.

Effective Date of the Executive Directive  
 
This Executive Directive shall become effective upon its signing and shall remain in full 
force and effect until amended or rescinded by further executive order.  
 
 
Given under my hand and under the Seal of the Commonwealth of Virginia this 17th day of 
April 2026. 
 
 
 
  
       Abigail D. Spanberger, Governor 
  
 
